  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Initial SMB Authentication with Local Admin", "technique": "T1078.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 09:14:07 CEST, a burst of SMB authentication attempts originated from internal host 10.108.57.5 toward 10.108.57.10 on TCP 445 using the local account 'administrator'. Suricata logged the 445 sessions (data.srcip=10.108.57.5, data.dstport=445) and Windows Security on the target logged logon attempts for targetUserName 'administrator' with logonType 3 (network). The same 'administrator' credential — a LOCAL account, not a domain account — was presented, indicating the attacker extracted a local admin password/hash from a previously compromised workstation and is now reusing it. Rule 5710 fired for the SMB auth failures, rule 18152 fired for the local-admin SMB access pattern.", "chain_reason": "Suricata + Windows Security correlate on data.dstport=445 with data.win.eventdata.targetUserName='administrator' and data.win.eventdata.logonType='3' from a single data.srcip=10.108.57.5. Local 'administrator' SMB auth to ADMIN$/C$ is the canonical pass-the-hash / local-credential-reuse lateral movement signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:14:07+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / SMB Spread Across Multiple Hosts", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "Between 09:16:41 and 09:21:48 CEST, the SAME source 10.108.57.5 using the SAME local 'administrator' account fanned out to three additional hosts — 10.108.57.177, 10.108.57.184, 10.108.57.198 — all on TCP 445, all logonType 3. Each target logged network logon attempts for targetUserName 'administrator'. The rapid sequential fan-out (one new host every ~2-3 minutes) from one source with one reused local credential is textbook SMB admin-share lateral movement (T1021.002) — the attacker is walking the subnet with the harvested local admin password.", "chain_reason": "Identical data.win.eventdata.targetUserName='administrator' + data.win.eventdata.logonType='3' + data.dstport=445 observed across data.dstip 10.108.57.177, 10.108.57.184, 10.108.57.198 — all sourced from the single data.srcip=10.108.57.5 within a 5-minute window. Same-credential reuse across multiple machines is the defining marker of local-account lateral spread.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:16:41+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / ADMIN$ Share Access and Remote Enumeration", "technique": "T1021.002", "what_happened": "At 09:24:30 CEST the attacker escalated from C$ probing to ADMIN$ share access attempts and RPC enumeration (rpcclient-style enumdomusers) against the reachable hosts, still as local 'administrator' over TCP 445. ADMIN$ access is a higher-privilege indicator than C$ — it is the share used by PsExec-class remote-execution tooling. The presence of ADMIN$ mount attempts plus remote user enumeration indicates the attacker is preparing remote code execution / credential harvesting on the lateral targets, not merely browsing files.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security on the targets logged additional logonType 3 events for targetUserName 'administrator' correlated with rule 18152 (local-admin SMB access) at the ADMIN$ stage. ADMIN$ + rpcclient enumeration over 445 from the same data.srcip=10.108.57.5 is the pre-execution staging step of SMB lateral movement.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T09:24:30+0200"}
